# 5b piece 1 — four mutations, never committed, each reverted (both files compared byte-for-byte after)
# command per mutation: swift test --filter AppleSessionTests   (this Mac: macOS 26.6.1, Xcode 27, Swift 6.4)
#
# M1  SessionKeeper: the busy guard removed        (if !kept.busy, kept.holds == history  →  if kept.holds == history)
#     convicted by: "a session whose answer was cut is never asked again..." (AC-306's core)
# M2  AppleToolAdapter: the yes captured at birth   (route → a copy of the route's state at init)
#     convicted by: "a tool made once for the session honours each answer's own yes"
# M3  SessionKeeper: the identity guard removed     (the .aside line deleted)
#     convicted by: both AC-309 rows
# M4  SessionKeeper: the check AFTER the stream removed (try Task.checkCancellation() before finished(...))
#     SURVIVES — nine of nine green. It is a belt: equality is the primary guard. An answer the vendor
#     finished but whose listener was cut is written by the memory as interrupted (or refused, when no
#     word got through), so the next history never equals what the session holds. Kept, and said so.

